      "math_academy_id": "q-17712",
      "knowledge_point_id": "039f9f0b-70dd-5360-9240-2260ff706c93",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Coefficient in a Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial",
      "problem": "What is the coefficient of $(x-2)^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)={e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ at $x=2?$",
      "worked_solution": "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(2)}{2}(x-2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-2)^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(2).\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-2{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+4{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2) & =-2{e}^{-{2}^{2}}+4\\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-4}+16{e}^{-4} \\\\  & =14{e}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x-2)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(2)}{2} & =\\frac{14{e}^{-4}}{2}=7{e}^{-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-4}"
            },
