      "math_academy_id": "q-50682",
      "knowledge_point_id": "33159d0b-24b5-587e-89e1-b545c2be59c4",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial of a Given Function About a Point",
      "problem": "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+x}}$ about $x=0$ is",
      "worked_solution": "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+x}}=(1+x)^{-1/2} & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(1+x)^{-3/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{3}{4}(1+x)^{-5/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =1+(-\\frac{1}{2})x+(\\frac{3}{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
